Z||000000||fotofrank||02-17-2001||08:49 PM|||| The port for the O2 secsor on the Hooker <BR>Tri Y header is farther from the engine than the stock header. I have heard that moving the port(O2 sensor) closer to the engine on the Hooker header is desireable. Any one know why? Also is it true that if the O2 sensor is too close or too far from the engine it "burns" up? Thanks in advance. <BR>   <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-17-2001||11:10 PM||||Another bogus idea spread by people that don't know any better.... <P>The problem is the new sensor location may be too cold to keep the O2 sensor operational. The sensor needs a certain amount of heat to work.<P>Leave the O2 where it is on the header. If you have O2 sensor problems with the header you'll have to change to a heated O2.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||02-19-2001||10:52 PM||||When I had my Tri-Y on an 87 coupe, I had no problems what so ever!||||reg||1
Z||000003||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-20-2001||03:01 AM||||Where can the Hooker Tri Y header be purchased?||||reg||1
